  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nRest of Today/Tonight: Showers will gradually expand in coverage \nthis afternoon as a surface low and the right rear quadrant of a jet \nstreak pass over the region. A few embedded thunderstorms are \npossible as well this afternoon, mainly across central WI as \nsoutherly flow south of stalled warm front bring weak elevated \ninstability to those areas. As the low exits the region tonight \nshowers will come to an end from north to south with a surge of much \ndrier air moving into the region.\n\nQuiet weather returns for next week: For the long term period from \nSunday through Friday, a prolonged stretch of quiet and dry weather \nis anticipated across north-central and northeast Wisconsin. \nShortwave energy originating from a western trough will slowly \nmigrate across the central Plains and southern Great Lakes toward \nthe East Coast during the week, keeping deep moisture and rain \nchances suppressed well south of the local area. Strong high \npressure centered over northern Ontario and Quebec will maintain an \nexpansive plume of dry air across northern Wisconsin, keeping rain \nprobabilities below 10 percent through Saturday. Under clear to \npartly cloudy skies, efficient radiational cooling on Monday and \nTuesday nights will drop overnight low temperatures into the mid to \nupper 30s across north-central Wisconsin, leading to patchy frost \nformation in low-lying areas. A steady warming trend will take place \nthroughout the week as afternoon high temperatures rise from the low \nand mid 60s on Monday and Tuesday into the low and mid 70s by Friday.",
